{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5408b6ecce5de02","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tNot below threshold: the strategy spans three components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI,\n15\tDashboard) owned by three distinct teams, and contains a genuine unknown (the GA\n16\tscope itself is undefined until HLR-1's assessment lands — the strategy's only\n17\topen question states this explicitly). Not documentation-only: HLR-1 through\n18\tHLR-5 require code, integration, and assessment work. Full decomposition follows.\n19\t\n20\tOwner teams are not stated in the strategy (see Health Warnings); they are\n21\tinferred from the architecture context files' Owning team fields:\n22\tRHOAI GenAI (Llama Stack), TrustyAI (TrustyAI), RHOAI Dashboard (Dashboard).\n23\t\n24\t## Epic List\n25\t\n26\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n27\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n28\t| E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n29\t| E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n30\t| E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n31\t| E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported Llama Stack integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n32\t| E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n33\t| E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift and TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Investigation)\"]\n40\t    E002[\"E002 API stability guarantee + upgrade path\"]\n41\t    E003[\"E003 Production support posture\"]\n42\t    E004[\"E004 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration\"]\n43\t    E005[\"E005 Dashboard default enablement\"]\n44\t    E006[\"E006 GA documentation uplift + migration notes (docs-authoring)\"]\n45\t    E001 --> E002\n46\t    E001 --> E003\n47\t    E002 --> E006\n48\t    E003 --> E006\n49\t    E004 --> E006\n50\t    E005 --> E006\n51\t```\n52\t\n53\t## DAG Justification\n54\t\n55\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n56\t|------|------|-----------|\n57\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The assessment (HLR-1) produces the definitive GA scope; the size and content of the API stability and hardening work is explicitly an estimate until it lands (strategy open question 1). True gate: scope/approach of the stability work may be rewritten. |\n58\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | SLO targets and the supported configuration matrix depend on assessed scale limits and error-handling gaps; the support posture's content changes with the assessment outcome. True gate: rewrite. |\n59\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics) | GA documentation must describe the stabilized, versioned API surface and the tested upgrade path. |\n60\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 | Migration and support documentation must reflect the published SLO targets, backport policy, and supported configuration matrix. |\n61\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 | The TrustyAI guardrails integration must be documented as supported in the GA docs. |\n62\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 | GA documentation must reflect default Dashboard enablement behavior for entitled clusters. |\n63\t\n64\tNo edge between E004/E005 and E001/E002/E003: the TrustyAI integration promotion\n65\t(HLR-4) and the Dashboard default-enablement change (HLR-5) are fully specified by\n66\ttheir HLRs, live in different repos owned by different teams, and share no\n67\tbuild-against artifacts with the Llama Stack work (the Dashboard integrates via\n68\tKubernetes CRs, not Llama Stack APIs). They run in parallel with the assessment\n69\tand deliver independent partial value while it runs.\n70\t\n71\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n72\t\n73\t| HLR | Priority | Epic |\n74\t|-----|----------|------|\n75\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 |\n76\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 |\n77\t| HLR-3 | P0 | E003 |\n78\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E004 |\n79\t| HLR-5 | P1 | E005 |\n80\t| HLR-6 | P2 | E006 |\n81\t\n82\tAll six HLRs are covered; none is orphaned. Each HLR maps to exactly one epic —\n83\tno priority bundling or splitting decisions were needed. HLR-6's epic is P0 (not\n84\tP2) because of the docs-authoring priority exception: the strategy frontmatter\n85\tpriority is Critical, which maps to P0; this does not push priority upstream\n86\tthrough Rule 11 edges.\n87\t\n88\t## Health Warnings\n89\t\n90\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy provides no\n91\t   Affected Components table with owner teams (a bare component list), no\n92\t   Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance\n93\t   Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from the\n94\t   architecture context files; epic acceptance criteria were derived from the\n95\t   HLRs. Human verification of the team assignments is recommended.\n96\t2. **GA scope unknown until E001 lands:** the strategy's own open question\n97\t   states the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate until the\n98\t   assessment produces the definitive GA scope. E002 and E003 are gated on E001\n99\t   for this reason; their effort estimates carry that uncertainty.\n100\t3. **Docs epic priority:** E006 is P0 solely via the docs-authoring priority\n101\t   exception (strategy priority Critical → P0). This is not a priority inversion\n102\t   and does not make the implementation epics it depends on P0 (they already\n103\t   are, from their own HLRs).\n104\t4. **Component mapping note:** E006 (docs-authoring) is filed under the\n105\t   canonical component `Llama Stack` with the RHOAI GenAI team — the\n106\t   documentation is Llama Stack GA documentation and the strategy names no\n107\t   documentation team. The canonical `Documentation` component was not used\n108\t   because no owner team for it is identifiable from the strategy or context.\n109\t5. **E002 size — one logical change with conditional re-split trigger:** E002\n110\t   bundles API versioning, deprecation-policy authoring, gap remediation,\n111\t   upgrade-path implementation, and automated upgrade verification. Effort\n112\t   estimate: unknown until E001 (the GA-readiness assessment) lands;\n113\t   provisionally at the ~2-week epic guideline. Why not split: these are\n114\t   tightly coupled facets of HLR-2's single deliverable (the stability\n115\t   guarantee on the published API surface) — one logical change; splitting by\n116\t   sub-deliverable would duplicate the API-surface audit and fragment the\n117\t   guarantee. Conditional re-split trigger: if the E001 assessment classifies\n118\t   the blocking API-stability gap set as multi-sprint work, re-split E002\n119\t   along the remediation vs upgrade-path seams (gap remediation and API\n120\t   versioning/deprecation in one epic; upgrade-path implementation plus its\n121\t   automated verification in the other).\n122\t\n123\t## Tiered Delivery\n124\t\n125\tNo external dependency with uncertain timing exists in this strategy, so no\n126\tTier 1 / Tier 2 split applies. Note, however, that E004 and E005 are independent\n127\tof the E001 assessment and proceed immediately in parallel — they constitute\n128\tdeliverable-early work while the gating assessment runs.\n129\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8324dda922943b0","agent_id":"ad5f15da8fef298da","session_id":"1249e1cd-8cf6-43c9-b6fd-d1e7ae430a4a","uuid":"2419b2f9-894d-4804-b40e-3f948fa54f77","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:16:45.817Z","tool_result_m